.TH httrack 1 "@DATE@" "httrack website copier"
.SH NAME
httrack \- offline browser : copy websites to a local directory
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B httrack [ url ]... [ \-filter ]... [ +filter ]...
@SYNOPSIS@
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B httrack
allows you to download a World Wide Web site from the Internet to a local directory, building recursively all directories, getting HTML, images, and other files from the server to your computer. HTTrack arranges the original site's relative link-structure. Simply open a page of the "mirrored" website in your browser, and you can browse the site from link to link, as if you were viewing it online. HTTrack can also update an existing mirrored site, and resume interrupted downloads.
.SH EXAMPLES
@EXAMPLES@
.SH OPTIONS
@OPTIONS@
.SH EXIT STATUS
.IP 0
The mirror ran to the end, or stopped because it was asked to: a signal, the
interactive shell, or a transfer budget set with
.I \-\-max\-time
or
.IR \-\-max\-size .
Meeting a budget is the outcome that was asked for, not a failure, so it is not
an abort even though the log file calls it one.
.IP 3
A mirror started and the engine gave up before the end: a full disk, a cache
write failure, or a link table that hit the
.I \-#L
cap. Unlike a time or size budget, that cap cuts short work that was asked for
rather than granting it, which is why the two kinds of user-set limit report
differently. The reason is in the log file, and what was mirrored before the
abort is kept. This is the process exit status; the engine's own
.I exit_xh
is a separate channel with its own values, and the two are kept apart so that
neither can be read as the other.
.IP "1, 255"
.B httrack
refused the command line, or a cache operation it asked for failed. No mirror ran.
.SH FILES
.I /etc/httrack.conf
.RS
The system wide configuration file.
.RE
.TP
.I cookies.txt
The cookie jar, in Netscape format. Without
.IR \-\-cookies\-file ,
the mirror reads the one in its output directory and the one in the current
directory, and writes the jar back to its output directory at the end. With
.IR \-\-cookies\-file ,
that file alone is the jar. HTTrack rewrites it at the end and keeps its old
content as
.IR <file>.bak .
HTTrack leaves the file unchanged and warns if a line could not be loaded or a
cookie repeats. It does the same when the file holds a comment that no known
tool writes. An empty expiry field means a session cookie.
.IP
The file given to
.I \-\-cookies\-file
can also hold a document.cookie string, such as
.IR "a=1; b=2" ,
as a browser console prints it, or a pasted
.I Cookie:
request header. Those cookies are sent to the registrable domain of the first
start URL and its subdomains, or to that host alone when it is an address or a
public suffix. They are Secure when that URL is https. HTTrack never rewrites
such a file. HttpOnly cookies are not in it. A cookies.txt export has them,
and so does the Cookie header from DevTools "Copy as cURL", pasted here.
.TP
.I <name>.hts
A login bundle, named by a start URL that ends with
.IR #httrack\-bundle=<name> ,
such as
.IR https://example.com/members/#httrack\-bundle=login1 .
The name holds letters, digits,
.IR . ,
.I _
and
.I \-
only. HTTrack reads the file in the project folder, or in the folder given to
.IR "\-\-bundle\-dir <dir>" ,
and never writes it. doit.log keeps that folder as an absolute path. HTTrack
reads the suffix on the command line, in a
.I \-%L
list and in doit.log, never on a crawled link. A missing file, or one that
does not parse, stops the run before any request.
.IP
The first line is
.IR "# HTTrack bundle 1" .
Then come
.I url
lines, each one more start URL, at most one
.I ua
line, and
.I header
and
.I scope
lines. A
.I cookies
line ends them, and a Netscape cookie jar follows it.
.IP
The bundle's user agent and headers replace
.I \-F
and
.I \-%X
for each host in its scope, and only there. A bundle with header lines but no
.I ua
line sends the
.I \-F
user agent. When any start URL naming the bundle, or any of its url lines, is
https, its headers go over https alone, and so do those of the bundles it
merges with. By default the scope is the registrable domains of the start
URLs naming the bundle and of its url lines. A host with no registrable domain,
such as a public suffix, a single label or an address, covers itself alone.
Scope lines replace that default. HTTrack drops a cookie outside the scope and
gives the count in one warning. HTTrack refuses a Host, Cookie, Proxy\-* or
framing header, a scope line naming a public suffix, and a line that holds a
control byte or is too long, in the cookie jar too and even under
.IR \-b0 .
A second
.I ua
line, header lines over 8192 bytes, or a full cookie jar stops the run too. A
repeated cookie line or an unknown key only warns, and so does an unknown #
line in the cookie jar. Before the
.I cookies
line, a # line is a comment. Messages give the file and the line number, never
a value. Two bundles whose
scopes overlap must give the same user agent, headers and cookie values, or the
run stops.
.IP
The bundle's cookies go into the cookie jar, which HTTrack writes at the end of
the crawl. Of the bundle and the jar, the newer file wins a cookie both hold.
A new capture therefore wins over the jar, and a cookie the server refreshed
wins over an older bundle. On equal times the bundle wins.
.PP
A running mirror also watches its own output directory for these. Creating one
is how a script drives an engine it has no other handle on. On Windows, which
has no cross\-process
.BR kill (1),
it is the only way.
.TP
.I hts\-in_progress.lock
Written when the mirror starts and deleted when it ends. It records the command
line, and names the two request files below.
.TP
.I hts\-stop.lock
Create it to pause the mirror. The engine deletes it, lets the transfers already
in flight finish, writes
.I hts\-paused.lock
and waits. Delete that one to carry on.
.TP
.I hts\-abort.lock
Create it to stop the mirror and keep what it has downloaded, so that a later
.I \-\-continue
resumes from there rather than starting over. The engine deletes it and stops.
.PP
The engine acts on either request only when the file is stamped later than
.IR hts\-in_progress.lock ,
which the mirror dates one second back when it starts. A request created after
the mirror started is therefore heard at once, on every filesystem. One left
behind by an earlier run is ignored.
A request the engine cannot delete is ignored too, because it would otherwise
fire on every later poll.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.IP HOME
Is being used if you defined in /etc/httrack.conf the line
.I path ~/websites/#
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Errors/Warnings are reported to
.I hts\-log.txt
by default, or to stderr if the
.I \-v
option was specified.
.SH LIMITS
@LIMITS@
@FOOTER@
.PP
The graphical interface is
.BR webhttrack (1).
